A||||11||SCCA FIERO||Wheel bearing seal.....Where could one be??
Z||000000||SCCA FIERO||06-11-99||08:01 PM||||Yesterday I decide to replace the wheel stud I broke the other day on my 88 coupe. I had to pull the hub off to get the stud in from behind. When I did this some dirt fell in the bearings. I then decided to pull the bearings out and clean them and repack them. I took the seal out and cleaned everything up. I then called Checker to get a new seal. They had it, I bought it, I took it home.....it doesn't fit! I then called every place in town. NO LUCK! All I can find is the hub kit...$60.00. I even looked in the Fiero Store catalog....nothing but the whole assembly again. I can't beleive this. The seal is about a 4 or 5 dollar part and it's gonna cost me 60 bucks. Any ideas???<p>Chris...88 coupe, 86SE V6
Z||000001||RFMike||06-11-99||08:10 PM||||I changed bearings and seals in my 86 GT.  Got the parts at a local Big Wheel store.  Don't know if an 88 is the same.  The seals were about 3.50 apiece.  They can probably ship, but you'd be waiting if they aren't in your area.  Good luck.<br>Mike
Z||000002||ezsurf||06-11-99||08:11 PM||||SCCA Fiero,<br>Must be a special size on the 88.  I had no problem finding front seals on my 86 SE.  
Z||000003||SCCA FIERO||06-11-99||09:12 PM||||Yeah the 88 is TOTALLY different. No problem finding the parts for my 86SE V6 in fact they gave me one of those. It didn't work. 
Z||000004||SCCA FIERO||06-11-99||09:25 PM||||The 88 is TOTALLY different. I think they gave me a seal for one of the older cars. Of course it didn't fit...
Z||000005||SCCA FIERO||06-11-99||09:27 PM||||HMMM....I looked after I posted the first reply. It wasn't there so I did another one....now it's up. Weird...
Z||000006||lowCG||06-11-99||10:08 PM||||Are there any numbers on the old seal?Probably are,and are useable in finding a replacement,either by cross referencing,or more likely by using the same number and ordering another.Bower/BCA bearing catalogs have cross reference guides in the back.<br>I was charged $30+ for one(inner)of the wheel brgs. on the front of my '86,because it wasn't a standard race,just saw one at some website for five bucks!<br>
Z||000007||SCCA FIERO||06-11-99||10:48 PM||||I got the seal # off a hub kit at Checker. The old one was destroyed but the #'s that remained matched the new ones on the hub assembly at Checker. They tried to cross referance them but found nothing. The stupid seal I need was in my hand in the hub kit but I can't get just that seal. 
Z||000008||PaUL||06-12-99||01:34 AM||||yeah thats one thing bad about the 88. Even though it is a better car, the fact that only a few were produced makes the parts scarce. Also I dont think the 88 shares as much parts with other GM cars as the earlier models do. 
Z||000009||lowCG||06-12-99||11:39 AM||||Yuck,sounds like a foreign car problem,from my past cars.Time to roll up your sleeves,take off your coat,and take a look in your local yellow pages under Hydraulic Equipment and supplies,find a place that can get seals by size and type(there are many).I've rebuilt Lancia suspension struts,which have some unique,and very bitchin' valving inside.Couldn't have done it without these suppliers.They're out there!
Z||000010||theogre||06-12-99||03:43 PM||||try GM?<p>Is there a logo, as well as the numbers? that might tell you something. not that many companies make that stuff for GM.
Z||000011||GREENDANO||06-13-99||07:52 AM||||If I recall correctly, 88's use sealed bearings at all four corners like the rear bearings in a Pre-88.  The seal you want/need is not a bearing seal but rather a knuckle or spindle dust seal.  <br>  Tell the parts dude (or dude-et) to look in a catalog maybe near drivshafts.  I got new knuckle-driveshaft seals with my new rear hub and bearing assemblies this past winter.<br>  Dan-O
